 storage is a bound and at the upper cap can exceed the archive on single streams. The two boundary tests (misaligned schedules and shifted boundaries) were added in response to review and registered before they were run. The registered criterion of the misaligned schedules was MICE above reset on at least 10 of the 12 streams with a mean gain of at least one point; that of the shifted boundaries was MICE above reset on at least 7 of the 8 with a mean gain of at least one point. Both failed. The cache holds every expert's prediction for every batch; the fast mixture of batch $t$ is formed from these with weights that depend on the labels of earlier batches only, so replaying the rule over the cache issues the same predictions an online run would. All reported numbers for \method{} come from such replays: the method above with $\eta_2=\tfrac12$ (\texttt{simulate2(outer='brier', scale=0.5, temper=True)} in the released code, frozen on 2026-10-05), and the stated variants with the log-loss or with $\eta_2=10$ as replays of the same rule with those parameters. The runs that produced the cache emitted a different, single-level mixture, which we do not report. All constants were set on synthetic development streams; none was tuned on a real stream. Hypotheses, data and decision rules were written down before each test, and every test stream was run once. The real streams were opened in five stages (\cref{tab:real}). The first three were also used, after their one-time evaluation, to develop the slow level. The third held-out set (eight later segments of four streams) and the grid seeds 20 and 21 were evaluated after the two-level rule was frozen with a step of 10; the step $\tfrac12$ required by \cref{prop:outer} was added to that analysis before any result was computed. The fourth held-out set, six further segments that no method had loaded, was then run once with \method{} exactly as defined in \cref{sec:method} as the registered method (\cref{app:prereg}). The registered predictions used a simplification of the area term: a stored context of $M$ rows on every recurrence, whereas \cref{prop:recall} has $n_c=\min(M,(c-1)L)$, i.e.\ 500 rows on the second visit when $L=500$. Recomputed with $n_c$ from the same learning curves, the predictions change only for $L=500$ (100 centroids: 9.2 instead of 10.4 points; 30 centroids: 1.7 instead of 1.9), the rank correlations are unchanged (0.94 and 0.83) and the magnitudes agree more closely (9.2 versus 8.6). We report the registered values in \cref{tab:grid,fig:recall} because they were fixed before the runs. The two seed pairs answer different questions. The curves were measured on the concepts of seeds 10 and 11, so there the prediction is for concepts already sampled, using additional labelled data from them; we also found that this data repeats part of the random draws of the evaluated seed-10/11 streams (\cref{app:grid}), and curves remeasured on independent rows give the same rank correlations (0.94 and 0.83) and predictions within 0.6 points. Seeds 20 and 21 share no data with the curves: there the curves of other concept draws from the same generator predict the gains, which is the evidence for transfer to new concepts of the same family, with two data seeds. Every stage was run once when it was opened; the third and fourth held-out sets were opened after the   two-level rule was frozen. The oracle test of \cref{tab:oracle} was registered with its seeds and criteria before it was run: the measured area term within 1.0 point of the visit-specific prediction on at least five of the six cells with a mean absolute difference of at most 0.5 points, and a Spearman correlation of at least 0.8; both held. Its TFM uses backbone seed 0, the learning curves seeds 10 and 11.
